  "query_preview": "..A trough was centered just off the California coast \nwith high pressure centered over the southern United States \nFriday afternoon. The southerly flow between these two pressure \nsystems continued to pull monsoon moisture northward. Northwest \nArizona was seeing the most activity resulting from being closer \nto cooler mid-level temperatures and weak upward motion. The main \nobservation through the early afternoon was generally fast moving \nstorm cells locally training in spots across northwest Arizona. \nIn addition, vertical shear associated with the trough resulted \nin a few organized and severe thunderstorms in the area of Chino \nValley. A Flash Flood Watch remains in effect for portions of \nnorthwest Arizona until 8 PM MST.\n\nFor tonight...The current pattern remains in place. Storms will be\nmost numerous across northwest portions of Arizona and generally\nisolated elsewhere. Isolated activity will then continue here and\nthere through the night. The threat for flash flooding will \nremain through mid-evening across northwest portions of Arizona. \nHeavy downpours and briefly severe behavior could be observed with\nany storm this evening, most likely across the northwest, \nenhanced by cooler temperatures and wind shear aloft.\n\nFrom Saturday into Tuesday...The high pressure center gradually \nbuilds from over the southern United States to over Arizona.\nMonsoon moisture will remain at the lower limits for activity for\nmost of the area as well as warmer temperatures aloft adding a \nstabilizing aspect to the atmosphere. The best chance for showers \nand storms area wide will be on Saturday as cooler air aloft, \nvertical shear and even weak upward motion associated with the \nCalifornia trough remains in place. Beyond Saturday as the high\nbuilds, warming temperatures aloft take over for much of northern\nArizona for generally an isolated threat. The exception, moisture\nvalues will remain just high enough over the high terrain of far \neastern Arizona for scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms, \nprimarily the White Mountains region.\n\nLooking further out...It appears active monsoon weather will \nreturn as the high pressure center finds it's way to the Four \nCorners region, opening the door to increasing monsoon moisture.",
